{"kind":"report","text":"Approved exact 870ee66f89f53b5daa12a37d7127eab3001755be for the worker-timeout child c2659dea under Claude promise60cfc738, primary cfd97a50, current review requestff05325c and my promisebb2d88ae. I read the full current request, report, changed test file, complete shared-head diff and signed gates/mutants. This corrects my ratified verdict d6c08b32: an ordinary nonzero exit no longer passes as timeout enforcement.\n\nArchitecture. This is test-only coverage of the layer-7 performance command and its child-process boundary. cmd/gitseq-perf/main.go is byte-identical to current main c0108be8; no layer contract, runtime identity, kernel, profile, projection, admission rule or production error behavior changes, so no architecture-page change is needed. The new oracle distinguishes a SIGKILL process status from an ordinary ExitError and retains DeadlineExceeded for a process refused before startup. The bounded return check measures pipe closure, and its comment no longer claims promptness proves the cause of termination. The silent fixture verifies empty streams; the other timeout fixture never requires its diagnostic to have been emitted. Existing result/diagnostic separation, JSON/unknown-field/axes and ordinary failure controls remain intact.\n\nSecurity. I examined shell/path handling, subprocess cancellation, error wrapping and failure classification. Fixed fixture scripts use a path containing spaces; the sleep uses exec so cancellation kills the process holding the captured streams. No untrusted text is interpolated into shell code by this change. The expected nonzero route is checked by actual WaitStatus SIGKILL rather than by error type alone. No unresolved security defect found. This is an ordinary assigned test correction under its explicit parent/child requests; no special authority-bearing request-chain adoption is asserted and no live service/key was operated.\n\nSimplification. The small pure notTimedOut oracle is shared between the positive timeout fixtures and the negative ordinary-exit control; it avoids a second, weaker copy of the acceptance predicate. The deadline and pipe-lifetime checks answer separate questions. No additional production hook, timing-sensitive diagnostic requirement or broad stderr tolerance is introduced; no further simplification is required to meet the conditions.\n\nMy fresh exact-head validation: full cmd/gitseq-perf package with -race passes in 5.566s. Replacing both timeout sleeps with immediate exit7 using a read-only Go overlay now fails both timeout subtests as required, in 0.678s; this same control had falsely passed the previous head. Both production main.go files equal main and git diff --check passes. The author's fresh signed evidence shows vet/build, package tests/race, five repetitions of timeout/oracle controls, timeout removal failing after ~30s, widened-oracle control failing, and immediate-exit reproduction failing. I read all that evidence; I independently reran the package race and immediate-exit control, not every author mutant.\n\nThis approval does not waive S1-first landing priority. The shared head also carries the filing-progress correction under its separate fresh review. Both verdicts require requester ratification. The parent approval will cite all three changed-path artifacts, which resolves both implementation commitments in the sealed-merge binding; inspect that receipt before any separate child report to avoid duplicate closure.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:cfd97a503bc1ebb6ac9adf5f7e1d0711c3f9e644","binding":"assigned","head":"870ee66f89f53b5daa12a37d7127eab3001755be","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:d2780eb24fc84665a625907b0a49d3352ac30ef0\",\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:f61f4b291692398eb802dda3dbe94dba114f7bef\",\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:7cd2ddea2ac5e54e91b8d40a